data: {"id":"chatcmpl-20261013163744750468156c955d568prmLttS2","created":1791909465,"object":"chat.completion.chunk","model":"glm-5.3-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_247ac8e42ec749b09373ce6e","index":0,"type":"function","function":{"name":"write","arguments":"{\"content\":\"BOUNDS AND COVERAGE OF THE VERIFICATION\\n=======================================\\n\\nClaim: For all integers x, (x + 1) * (x - 1) = x^2 - 1\\nSpec (spec.txt): forall x : Z. (x + 1) * (x - 1) = x * x - 1\\n\\n1. DOMAIN OF THE CLAIM\\n   The claim quantifies over ALL integers Z: an infinite (countably),\\n   unbounded domain in both directions. It makes no restriction to\\n   positive values, machine-width integers, or any finite range.\\n\\n2. PROOF METHOD AND ITS COVERAGE\\n   Method: deductive proof (proof.txt) from stated axioms\\n   (commutativity of + and *, distributivity, subtraction as addition\\n   of the additive inverse), applied to an arbitrary x, closed by\\n   universal generalization.\\n   Coverage: COMPLETE with respect to the axioms used. The derivation\\n   holds for every x : Z, including values outside any tested range,\\n   because no step depends on the particular value of x.\\n\\n   Qualification (from audit.txt): the proof additionally relies on\\n   ring facts of Z beyond the four permitted axioms - multiplicative\\n   unit (Z2), annihilator (Z3), additive unit (Z4), and associativity\\n   of + as a regrouping convention (Z5). Under the standard reading\\n   that these are part of the structure of Z, the proof is sound; under\\n   a strict \\\"four axioms only\\\" reading, the proof is incomplete.\\n\\n3. CHECKER METHOD AND ITS COVERAGE\\n   Method: brute-force testing (counterexample_check.py) of\\n   x = -100 .. 100 (201 values), comparing (x+1)*(x-1) with x*x - 1\\n   using exact Python integer arithmetic (no overflow).\\n   Coverage: FINITE and PARTIAL. It covers only 201 of the infinitely\\n   many integers. It can refute the claim (a single failure would be a\\n   genuine counterexample) but can never establish it: passing tests\\n   provide inductive evidence only, not proof.\\n\\n4. LIMITATIONS OF THE CHECKER\\n   - Range bound: values with |x| > 100 are untested; a hypothetical\\n     counterexample there would be missed.\\n   - Not a proof: agreement on a finite set does not imply agreement\\n     on Z.\\n   - Fixed range: the bounds -100 and 100 are hard-coded; rerunning\\n     with a different range changes coverage but never achieves\\n     completeness.\\n   - Oracle identity: the checker evaluates both sides as Python\\n     expressions; it verifies the identity as computed, which assumes\\n     Python's arbitrary-precision integers faithfully model Z (valid\\n     here, since only +, *, - are used and no overflow occurs).\\n   - Structural scope: the checker tests a single fixed equation; it\\n     does not parse or validate spec.txt, proof.txt, or the axioms.\\n\\n5. OVERALL ASSESSMENT\\n   - The deductive proof is the load-bearing verification; subject to\\n     the axiom qualification in audit.txt, it establishes the claim\\n     for all of Z.\\n   - The brute-force check is corroborating evidence only: it confirms\\n     the claim on [-100, 100] and found no counterexamples\\n     (201/201 matches), consistent with the proof.\\n   - Together: claim established for all integers, with empirical\\n     corroboration limited to the tested range.\\n\",\"filePath\":\"/home/agent/workspace/bounds.txt\"}"}}]}}]}

